.Dd July 30, 2026
.Dt CPROGRESS 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm cprogress
.Nd copy files with a progress bar
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl R | r
.Op Fl f
.Op Fl v
.Op Fl -progress Ns = Ns Ar auto | force | none
.Ar source_file target_file
.Nm
.Op Fl R | r
.Op Fl f
.Op Fl v
.Op Fl -progress Ns = Ns Ar auto | force | none
.Ar source_file ... target_directory
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
copies files the way
.Xr cp 1
does, and prints a wget-style progress bar while it works.
.Pp
In the first form,
.Ar source_file
is copied to
.Ar target_file .
In the second form, each
.Ar source_file
is copied into the existing
.Ar target_directory .
.Pp
Before copying,
.Nm
walks the sources to total up the bytes, so the percentage and estimated time
are accurate from the first frame. The scan reads metadata only.
.Pp
The display is two lines: an aggregate bar for the whole operation, and a bar
for the file being copied. Files smaller than 8 MiB show only their path,
because a bar that fills within a single read is noise rather than information.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl R , r
Copy directory hierarchies recursively. Symbolic links are recreated as
symbolic links rather than followed, matching the default behavior of
.Nm cp Fl R .
.Pp
Without
.Fl R ,
a symbolic link is followed and whatever it points at is copied, which is also
what
.Xr cp 1
does.
A link to a directory is therefore reported as a directory, and a dangling
link as a missing file.
.It Fl f
If a destination file cannot be opened for writing, remove it and try again.
.It Fl v
Print the name of each file as it is copied, as
.Dq source -> dest ,
on standard output.
Errors go to standard error.
.Xr cp 1
splits them the same way, so redirecting either stream behaves identically.
.It Fl h , -help
Print the manual and exit.
.It Fl -progress Ns = Ns Ar auto | force | none
Control the progress display.
.Ar auto ,
the default, draws the bar only when standard error is a terminal.
.Ar force
always draws it.
.Ar none
never does.
.El
.Sh UNSUPPORTED OPTIONS
The following
.Xr cp 1
options are not implemented and are rejected with an error rather than
ignored, so that a script relying on one fails visibly:
.Pp
.Dl Fl p Fl i Fl n Fl a Fl c Fl l Fl s Fl X Fl H Fl L Fl P Fl N Fl S Fl x
.Pp
Extended attributes and access control lists are not copied.
.Sh UNSUPPORTED FILE TYPES
Only regular files, directories, and symbolic links are copied.
A socket, FIFO, or device node is reported as an unsupported file type and
skipped; the rest of the tree is still copied, and the exit status is 1.
.Pp
.Xr cp 1
differs in both directions.
Under
.Fl R
it recreates FIFOs and device nodes.
Without
.Fl R
it opens the source and copies whatever bytes it yields, so
.Dq cp /dev/null file
truncates
.Ar file
where
.Nm
reports an error instead.
.Xr cp 1
skips sockets just as
.Nm
does, but it exits 0 in all of these cases;
.Nm
exits 1, deliberately, because it leaves more behind than
.Xr cp 1
does.
.Sh FILE PERMISSIONS
A newly created file or directory takes the source's permission bits masked by
the current umask. An existing destination file keeps its own permissions. Both
match
.Xr cp 1 .
.Pp
Ownership, timestamps, and BSD file flags are not preserved; that is what
.Nm cp Fl p
would provide, and
.Fl p
is not supported.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 0
All files were copied successfully.
.It 1
At least one file could not be copied.
.It 64
The command line was malformed.
.It 130
The copy was interrupted with SIGINT.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Copy a directory tree, replacing anything already at the destination:
.Pp
.Dl cprogress -Rf ~/Photos /Volumes/Backup/Photos
.Pp
Use it in place of
.Xr cp 1
everywhere.
The bar is suppressed when standard error is not a terminal, and diagnostics
and
.Fl v
lines use the same streams and wording
.Xr cp 1
uses, so a script sees what it expects \(em with the exceptions noted under
UNSUPPORTED OPTIONS and UNSUPPORTED FILE TYPES:
.Pp
.Dl alias cp=cprogress
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr cp 1
